      "comment": "Processes Windows message pump with optional idle callback function.\nThis function implements a standard Windows message loop that can optionally\nexecute a callback function during idle periods when no messages are pending.\nThe function handles accelerator key translation for two global accelerator\ntables and properly dispatches messages to their target windows.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize message processing flags (BVar5=1, iVar3=0)\n2. Enter main message loop\n3. Peek for available messages using PeekMessageA\n4. If no message available:\n   a. Call optional callback function if provided (non-null)\n   b. Increment callback parameter and invoke callback\n   c. If callback returns non-zero, exit with callback result\n   d. If global DAT_6fbae750 is zero, yield CPU with Sleep(0)\n5. If message available:\n   a. Get message with GetMessageA\n   b. If message retrieved successfully (non-zero result):\n      - Check primary accelerator table (DAT_6fbdc020)\n      - If exists, translate accelerator for current window\n      - If translation successful, skip to message processing\n      - Check secondary accelerator table (DAT_6fbdc25c)  \n      - If exists, translate accelerator for current window\n   c. Translate message for keyboard input processing\n   d. Dispatch message to appropriate window procedure\n6. Continue loop until GetMessageA returns 0 (WM_QUIT)\n7. Return 1 on normal exit\n\nParameters:\n- pfnCallback: Optional function pointer for idle processing callback\n  IMPLICIT: Takes int parameter (loop count), returns int (0=continue, non-zero=exit)\n\nReturns:\n- 0: Never returned in current implementation\n- 1: Normal exit when message loop terminates (WM_QUIT received) \n- callback_result: Non-zero value returned by callback function\n\nNote: Function uses 7 SSA temporary variables (iVar3, iVar4, BVar5, pHVar6, BVar1, pHVar2, ptVar7) \ncreated by decompiler optimization - these cannot be renamed as they are synthetic.\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Callback function receives incrementing counter as parameter\n- If callback returns non-zero and BVar5 is false, forces return 1\n- Sleep(0) called only when DAT_6fbae750 global flag is zero\n- Accelerator processing uses Ordinal_10027() to get target window handle\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n- 0x0: NULL values for window handle, message filtering\n- DAT_6fbae750: Global flag controlling CPU yield behavior\n- DAT_6fbdc020: Primary accelerator table handle\n- DAT_6fbdc25c: Secondary accelerator table handle",
