      "comment": "Process pixel color value and extract RGB components with optional rendering context.\nThis function handles color value processing through two different pathways based on\nthe processing mode flag and performs additional rendering context operations when\nneeded. The function takes a color value and extracts individual RGB components\nwhile also producing a processed result value through output parameters.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize nColorComponents buffer with low byte of nColorValue (nColorValue & 0xff)\n2. Check nProcessMode flag to determine processing path\n3. If nProcessMode != 0, call FUN_6fae8d30 for alternative color conversion\n4. Pass nColorValue and pointers to bytes 1-3 of nColorComponents buffer\n5. Store result in nResult variable and check if any RGB bytes are non-zero\n6. If nProcessMode == 0 or RGB bytes are zero, call Ordinal_10054 for standard processing  \n7. Pass nColorValue, nProcessMode pointer, and RGB component pointers\n8. Mask nResult to low byte (nProcessMode & 0xff)\n9. If all RGB bytes remain zero after processing, perform rendering context operations\n10. Call Ordinal_10927 with g_hRenderContext and RGB component pointers\n11. Call Ordinal_10926 with g_hRenderContext to get final nResult value\n12. Output nResult and RGB components through pointer parameters\n\nParameters:\nnColorValue - int: Input pixel/color value, low byte used for processing initialization\npResult - int*: Output parameter for processed result value from color operations  \npbRed - int*: Output parameter for red component (0-255 range expected)\npbGreen - int*: Output parameter for green component (0-255 range expected)\npbBlue - int*: Output parameter for blue component (0-255 range expected)  \nnProcessMode - int: Processing mode flag (0=standard path, non-zero=alternative path)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Function returns no value, results provided through output pointer parameters\nSuccess indicated by populated RGB components and result value in output parameters\nNo explicit error codes returned, function always completes processing",
