      "comment": "Initialize game difficulty system if not already active\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate system state - Check if three system flags are inactive (DAT_6fbe929c, DAT_6fbe9254, DAT_6fbe927c all zero)\n2. Store difficulty level - Set global difficulty to provided level (DAT_6fbe90e8 = nDifficultyLevel)\n3. Apply difficulty bounds - If level exceeds maximum (26), clamp to default value (9)\n4. Initialize system components - Call FUN_6fb5d910(1, 0) for subsystem setup\n5. Set timer value - Initialize countdown/duration to 600 (0x258)\n6. Reset state flags - Clear system status variables (DAT_6fbe9270=1, DAT_6fbe926c=0, DAT_6fbe9268=0)\n7. Finalize initialization - Call SetGlobalState(0) to complete setup\n8. Early exit - Return immediately if any system flag is already active\n\nParameters:\nnDifficultyLevel (int): Game difficulty level to set (valid range: 0-26, clamped to 9 if > 26)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value, function completes by side effect on global state\n\nSpecial Cases:\nEarly Return: If any system flag (DAT_6fbe929c, DAT_6fbe9254, DAT_6fbe927c) is non-zero, function exits immediately without initialization\nDifficulty Bounds: Input values > 26 (0x1A) are automatically clamped to default level 9\nDefault Level: Value 9 appears to be standard/normal difficulty when clamping occurs\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x1A (26): Maximum allowed difficulty level before clamping\n0x9 (9): Default difficulty level used when input exceeds maximum\n0x258 (600): Timer/countdown initialization value\n0x1 (1): Active state flag value for DAT_6fbe9270\n0x0 (0): Reset value for multiple system state variables\n\nError Handling:\nNo explicit error codes - function uses early return pattern\nState validation prevents re-initialization if system already active\nInvalid difficulty levels are silently corrected rather than rejected",
      "tags": ["PARAM_1"]
