      "comment": "Check if a player ID already exists in the global linked list to detect ID conflicts.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Retrieve the global linked list head pointer (g_pLinkedListHead)\n2. Return false immediately if the linked list is empty\n3. Iterate through each node in the linked list:\n   a. Reconstruct a 32-bit player ID from four consecutive padding bytes in the current node\n   b. Compare the reconstructed ID against the player ID at offset +8 in player slot data\n   c. Return true immediately if IDs match (conflict detected)\n   d. Advance to the next node via pNext pointer\n4. Return false if end of list reached without finding a match (ID is unique)\n\nParameters:\npPlayerSlot (PlayerSlotData *): Pointer to player slot data structure containing player ID at header+8 offset\n\nReturns:\ntrue: Player ID already exists in linked list (ID conflict detected)\nfalse: Player ID not found in linked list (ID is unique and safe to use)\n\nStructure Layout:\nLinkedListNode (52 bytes):\nOffset  Size  Field Name    Type     Description\n+0x00   4     (unknown)     varies   Node data\n+0x0C   4     padding1-4    byte[4]  Four padding bytes that form player ID when combined\n+0x30   4     pNext         ptr      Pointer to next node in linked list\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x08 (8): Offset in PlayerSlotData header to access player ID field\n0x0C (12): Offset in LinkedListNode to access first padding byte containing ID\n0x30 (48): Offset in LinkedListNode to access pNext pointer for list traversal\n\nSpecial Cases:\nEmpty list: Returns false immediately when g_pLinkedListHead is NULL\nSingle node: Loop executes once and compares single entry before returning\nByte order reconstruction: Four padding bytes are assembled in little-endian order to form 32-bit ID",
