      "comment": "Network packet handler for creating a monster unit on the client.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Extract unit creation parameters from packet data structure\n2. Call AllocateAndInitializeUnit with extracted parameters:\n   - dwUnitType from pPacketData+2 (4 bytes)\n   - dwUnitClass from pPacketData+6 (1 byte)\n   - wRoomX from pPacketData+7 (2 bytes)\n   - wRoomY from pPacketData+9 (2 bytes)\n   - nUnitMode from pPacketData+1 (1 byte)\n   - Reserved parameter = 0\n3. If allocation succeeds and unit type is monster (1), update graphics\n4. Return (no return value)\n\nParameters:\npPacketData (ECX) - Pointer to network packet data with monster creation info:\n  +0x00: byte - Packet ID (0x0B for create monster)\n  +0x01: byte - Unit mode/action state\n  +0x02: uint - Unit type identifier (should be 1 for monster)\n  +0x06: byte - Monster class ID\n  +0x07: ushort - Room X coordinate\n  +0x09: ushort - Room Y coordinate\n  Packet size: 11 bytes (0x0B)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Unit created and registered internally by AllocateAndInitializeUnit\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If allocation fails (returns NULL), no graphics update occurs\n- If allocated unit is not type 1 (monster), graphics update is skipped\n- This allows the function to be used for non-monster creation without side effects\n\nClassification: Callback/Handler - packet message handler in function pointer table at 0x6fbb027c\nMessage ID: 0x0B (11) - Create Monster packet",
