      "comment": "Initializes the wall square grid system for rendering and coordinate mapping.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate grid dimensions: X squares = (viewport_width / 0xa0) + 0xb, Y squares = (viewport_height / 0x50) + 0xc\n2. Calculate total squares needed: total = (X_count + Y_count - 1)^2\n3. If existing wall buffer exists and size matches, skip allocation; otherwise free and reallocate\n4. Allocate wall square buffer: total^2 * 9 * 4 bytes using Ordinal_10042(0x3c, 0)\n5. Store grid dimensions at pViewport+0xeab0 (total), +0xeab4 (X_count), +0xeab8 (Y_count)\n6. Check display mode: if Ordinal_10010() returns 0 use full viewport, otherwise apply 0x30 pixel border\n7. Calculate viewport rectangle and store in global rectangle buffer at 0x6fbf2200\n8. Clear viewport flags at pViewport+0xea9c and +0x38\n9. Zero-initialize entire wall square buffer (total^2 * 9 * 4 bytes)\n10. Get cursor position via Ordinal_10114 and adjust by -3 X and -Y_count offset\n11. Initialize each WallSquare structure with world coordinates in nested loops\n12. Setup render buffers: get unit buffer (FUN_6fb62880), display buffer (FUN_6fb634e0)\n13. Calculate and store display scaling: width/5, height/5, composite buffer\n14. Register draw callback via Ordinal_10049 with callback at LAB_6fae7580\n\nParameters:\npViewport (ViewportContext*): Pointer to viewport/context structure containing display bounds and buffer pointers\ndwFlags (uint): Configuration flags (stored but not actively used in current implementation)\n\nReturns:\nvoid: No return value, function configures global state and viewport structure",
