      "instructions": ["0x262F0|SUB|ESP, 0x328", "0x262F6|MOV|DL, byte ptr [0x6fbdc134]", "0x262FC|PUSH|EDI", "0x262FD|MOV|ECX, 0x40", "0x26302|XOR|EAX, EAX", "0x26304|LEA|EDI, [ESP + 0x125]", "0x2630B|MOV|byte ptr [ESP + 0x124], DL", "0x26312|STOSD.REP|ES:EDI", "0x26314|STOSW|ES:EDI", "0x26316|STOSB|ES:EDI", "0x26317|MOV|ECX, 0x40", "0x2631C|XOR|EAX, EAX", "0x2631E|LEA|EDI, [ESP + 0x21]", "0x26322|MOV|byte ptr [ESP + 0x20], DL", "0x26326|STOSD.REP|ES:EDI"],
      "comment": "Creates or opens an automap (.map or .ma%d) file for the current game level\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Initialize three 260-byte path buffers (szPath1, szPath2, szPath3) with zero\n2. Call Ordinal_10115 to prepare file system or locking state\n3. Check if global flag DAT_6fbe90ec is null to determine path construction mode\n4. If flag is null (simple mode):\n   a. Construct map path using format \"%s%s.map\" into szPath2\n   b. Open file with Ordinal_10107 (flags: 0x80 = CREATE)\n   c. Return 0xFFFFFFFF on failure\n5. If flag is non-null (complex mode):\n   a. Format path into szPath3 using \"%s%s\\\" with DAT_6fbe90ec\n   b. Call Ordinal_10114 to create directory or validate path\n   c. Format final map path into szPath2 using \"%s%s.map\" with szPath3 and DAT_6fbe910c\n   d. Open file with Ordinal_10107 (flags: 0x80 = CREATE)\n   e. If open fails, fall back to simple mode (goto LAB_6faf649f)\n   f. Copy szPath3 to szPath1 (260 bytes)\n6. Initialize automap metadata structure (24 bytes = 0x18):\n   - nStructSize = 0\n   - nStructType = 0\n   - dwSlotIndex = 0\n   - anSlotIds[0..3] = 0\n7. Read structure header with Ordinal_10110 (24 bytes into nStructSize address)\n8. Validate structure header (size == 0x18 and type == 0x0B):\n   a. Search anSlotIds[0..3] for current level ID (*DAT_6fbe9288)\n   b. If level ID not found:\n      - Calculate next slot index with modulo 4 operation\n      - Store level ID in anSlotIds[dwSlotIndex]\n      - Update dwSlotIndex with modulo result\n      - Construct indexed map filename \"%s%s.ma%d\" into szPath2\n      - Write structure with Ordinal_10108\n   c. If level ID found, jump to cleanup (LAB_6faf6575)\n9. If structure invalid (size != 0x18 or type != 0x0B):\n   a. Initialize structure: nStructType = 0x0B, dwSlotIndex = 1\n   b. Store first level ID: anSlotIds[0] = *DAT_6fbe9288\n   c. Loop 4 times:\n      - Construct indexed map filename \"%s%s.ma%d\" into szPath2\n      - Write structure with Ordinal_10108\n10. Seek to file start with Ordinal_10112(0, 0)\n11. Write structure header (24 bytes) with Ordinal_10111\n12. Close or finalize file handle with Ordinal_10109\n13. Construct final map path again into szPath2\n14. Reopen file with Ordinal_10107 and return file handle\n\nParameters:\n\nNone (implicit global dependencies: DAT_6fbe90ec, DAT_6fbe910c, DAT_6fbe9288)\n\nIMPLICIT: DAT_6fbdc134 - Drive letter or path prefix for all automap paths\n\nReturns:\n\nuint - File handle for opened automap file\n       0xFFFFFFFF (decimal -1) on file creation failure\n       Valid handle (>= 0) on success\n\nSpecial Cases:\n\n- If DAT_6fbe90ec is null, uses simple path construction without subdirectory\n- If file open fails in complex mode, falls back to simple mode\n- Slot ID array supports up to 4 concurrent level automap files\n- Modulo operation ensures slot index wraps around (0-3)\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n\n0x104 (260) - Maximum path length for Windows file paths (MAX_PATH)\n0x18 (24) - Size of automap metadata structure header\n0x0B (11) - Expected structure type identifier for automap metadata\n0x80 (128) - File creation flag (CREATE_ALWAYS or similar)\n0x80000003 - Bitmask for modulo 4 operation (preserves lower 2 bits)\n0xfffffffc - Twos complement adjustment mask for negative modulo results\n4 - Maximum number of automap slot IDs tracked\n\nError Handling:\n\n- Returns 0xFFFFFFFF if initial file open fails in simple mode\n- Falls back to simple mode if complex mode file open fails\n- No explicit error handling for Ordinal_10108/10111 write operations\n- Assumes Ordinal_10114 directory creation succeeds without validation",
