      "comment": "Renders the current date string to the video buffer for automap display.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Initialize 300 Unicode objects (600 bytes) by calling default constructor for each element\n2. Retrieve current date string from Ordinal_10013 (returns ANSI string)\n3. Convert ANSI date string to Unicode format using toUnicode with 300 character capacity\n4. Load current video string buffer pointer from g_dwVideoStringBufferPointer\n5. Write Unicode string to video buffer at offset 0x04 with flags 0x00 using Ordinal_10121\n6. Render the string to screen using Ordinal_10117 with buffer offset and flags\n7. Advance video string buffer pointer by 0x10 bytes for next string\n\nParameters:\n\nNone (void function)\n\nReturns:\n\nvoid\n\nSpecial Cases:\n\n- Unicode array size: 300 elements (600 bytes) to accommodate maximum date string length\n- Buffer advancement: Fixed increment of 0x10 (16) bytes regardless of actual string length\n- String flags: Always uses 0x00 for standard rendering mode\n- Buffer offset: Always writes to offset 0x04 within current buffer entry\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n\n0x12c (300 decimal) - Maximum Unicode character count for date string\n0x258 (600 decimal) - Stack space allocated for Unicode buffer (300 × 2 bytes)\n0x04 - Fixed offset within video buffer entry for string data\n0x00 - String rendering flags (standard mode)\n0x10 (16 decimal) - Video buffer entry size increment\n\nStructure Layout:\n\nVideo Buffer Entry (inferred):\nOffset  Size  Field Name           Type    Description\n------  ----  -------------------  ------  -----------\n0x00    4     dwEntryType          uint    Entry type identifier\n0x04    ?     UnicodeString        ushort* Unicode string pointer or inline data\n0x?     ?     (unknown fields)     ?       Additional entry data\nTotal: 0x10 bytes minimum",
