      "comment": "Processes keyboard binding configuration strings and registry operations.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize three 260-byte local buffers to zero using REP STOSD pattern (0x40 iterations)\n2. Load global configuration byte from DAT_6fbdc134 into each buffer's null terminator position\n3. Call Ordinal_10115 to initialize the registry/keyboard binding system\n4. Check if configuration flag DAT_6fbe90ec is set (non-zero value test)\n5. If flag is set: call Ordinal_578 to process string format with mask pattern \"%s %s %s\" at 6fbb1dbc\n6. Call Ordinal_10114 to commit and update registry entries after string processing\n7. Call Ordinal_578 again with key pattern \"%s %s %s key\" at address 6fbb2ecc\n8. Call Ordinal_10107 with nSecondParam and flags 0x80 to apply keyboard bindings\n9. If Ordinal_10107 returns success (EAX != -1), return early from function\n10. If flag not set or first binding failed: call Ordinal_578 with alternate string source\n11. Call Ordinal_10107 a second time with nSecondParam and flags 0x80\n12. Return to caller\n\nParameters:\nnFirstParam (ECX): Reserved/unused parameter, retained for API compatibility\nnSecondParam (EDX): Keyboard binding configuration identifier or registry key reference passed to Ordinal_10107\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Function performs registry and keyboard binding configuration updates as side effects only\n\nSpecial Cases:\nEarly return occurs only when DAT_6fbe90ec flag is set AND Ordinal_10107 succeeds (returns != -1). Uses fixed 0x104 (260 byte) buffer size. Stack temporaries abBuffer1, abBuffer2, abBuffer3 are phantom variables optimized by decompiler but visible in assembly at [ESP+0x214], [ESP+0xc], and [ESP+0x110].\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x40 - Loop iteration count for 64-element buffer initialization (0x40 * 4 = 0x100 + 4 = 0x104 bytes)\n0x104 - Buffer size 260 bytes for Ordinal_578 string processing parameters\n0x80 - Flags parameter to Ordinal_10107 (registry write or apply mode)",
