      "comment": "Checks if a key binding exists and is active for the given modifier and key code.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize binding index to 0 and entry pointer to key binding types table\n2. Loop through key binding entries (10-byte stride):\n   a. Compare modifier key at offset -6 from types pointer with input modifier\n   b. Compare key code at types pointer with input key code\n   c. If both match, exit loop and check if binding is active\n   d. Otherwise, advance pointer by 10 bytes and increment index\n   e. If pointer exceeds table end (0x6fbf5aa2), return false (no match)\n3. Return true if matched entry's command ID is not -1 (0xFFFF)\n\nParameters:\n- nModifierKey (ECX): Modifier key code (Ctrl, Alt, Shift combinations)\n- nKeyCode (EDX): Virtual key code to search for\n\nReturns:\n- true: Key binding exists and has a valid command ID assigned\n- false: No matching binding found or binding has invalid command (-1)\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0x6fbf562e: g_adKeyBindingTypes base address (offset +6 in entry)\n- 0x6fbf562c: g_aKeyBindingTable base address\n- 0x6fbf5aa2: Table end boundary (116 entries * 10 bytes + base)\n- 10 (0x0A): Key binding entry stride in bytes\n- -1 (0xFFFF): Invalid/unassigned command ID marker\n\nRelated Functions:\n- RegisterKeyboardBinding(): Adds new bindings to the table\n- LookupKeyBindingByCommandId(): Reverse lookup by command ID\n- SaveKeyboardBindingsToRegistry(): Persists bindings to registry",
