      "comment": "Looks up a keyboard binding by command and type, returning a localized string ID.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize table index to 0 and pointer to key binding types table\n2. Search g_adKeyBindingTypes for matching command and type pair\n3. Each table entry is 10 bytes: command (4 bytes at offset -6), type (4 bytes at offset 0)\n4. If no match found (pointer exceeds 0x6fbf5aa1), call Ordinal_10004 for default string\n5. On match, retrieve string ID from g_aKeyBindingTable at index*5 (10-byte stride / 2)\n6. If string ID is 0xFFFF (invalid marker), return default string via Ordinal_10004\n7. For string IDs < 0xE0, use switch table to map virtual key codes to string IDs:\n   - Cases 1-3: Mouse buttons, return default string\n   - Cases 4, 8, 9, 0xC-0x29, 0x2A-0x2F: Standard keys, use jump table\n   - Cases 0x5B-0x5D: Windows keys\n   - Cases 0x60-0x6F: Numpad keys\n   - Cases 0x70-0x87: Function keys F1-F24\n   - Cases 0x90-0x91: Num/Scroll Lock\n   - Cases 0xBA-0xC0, 0xDB-0xDE: OEM keys\n8. For string IDs 0xE0-0x104 (extended keys), use secondary jump table\n9. Default case: Store key code in g_szKeyBindingStringBuffer and return its address\n\nParameters:\nnBindingCommand - Key binding command ID (ECX, __fastcall)\nnBindingType - Key binding type/category (EDX, __fastcall)\n\nReturns:\nchar* - Pointer to localized string ID, or direct key code string buffer\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x6fbf5aa1 - End of key binding types table\n0xFFFF - Invalid/unassigned binding marker\n0xE0 - Threshold for extended key handling\n0x100-0x104 - Extended key string IDs\n0xEB2 - Default string ID (via Ordinal_10004)\n0xEB3-0xF14 - Virtual key to string ID mappings\n\nRelated Functions:\n- Ordinal_10004 (D2Lang.GetStringById) - Returns localized string by ID\n- RegisterKeyboardBinding - Registers new bindings in the table\n- LoadKeyboardBindings - Populates binding table from registry",
