      "comment": "Searches for a format specifier pattern in a Unicode string and performs string concatenation.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize a 400-character Unicode buffer on the stack (1600 bytes)\n2. Iterate through the input string character by character\n3. For each character, check if it's a format specifier ('%' = 0x25)\n4. If format specifier found, check if next character matches the digit specifier (0x30 + param_2)\n5. If both match, jump to string operations section\n6. If not a format specifier, copy character to output buffer and increment counter\n7. Continue until string length exhausted or format specifier found\n8. If format specifier found (at step 4-5), call string concatenation and string copy functions\n9. Return to caller\n\nParameters:\npwFormatString (short *): Input Unicode string to search for format specifiers\nnDigitSpecifier (int): ASCII digit to match after '%' character (0x30='0', 0x31='1', etc.)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No explicit return value\n\nSpecial Cases:\nMagic Numbers: 0x25 = '%' (format specifier marker), 0x30 = ASCII '0' (digit base), 0x20 = ' ' (space character)\nThe function works with 16-bit Unicode characters (wchar_t), advancing by 2 bytes per iteration\n\nNote: Function uses unaffected register parameters (ESI, EDI) for string pointers passed from caller",
