      "comment": "Processes player item state only when game is active and not in menu\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if game system is enabled via IsGameSystemEnabled\n2. If enabled (non-zero), return early - no processing during system active state\n3. Check if game is in menu state via IsGameInMenuState\n4. If in menu (non-zero), return early - no item processing during menus\n5. Check global game active flag (g_byGameActiveFlag) for value 0x01\n6. If flag is not 0x01, return early - game not in active gameplay state\n7. Get game context pointer via GetGameContext\n8. Extract item type/ID from context offset +0x84 (current inventory item)\n9. Call ProcessPlayerItemState with player context, item type, and item ID = 0\n\nParameters:\n  (none) - Uses global state and context pointers\n\nReturns:\n  void - All paths return without value\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Game system enabled: Skip processing (system busy or initializing)\n  - Menu state active: Skip processing (player in menu, not gameplay)\n  - Game active flag != 0x01: Skip processing (game paused or transitioning)\n  - All three conditions must pass for item processing to occur\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n  0x84 - Offset in game context structure for current item type/ID (dword)\n  0x01 - Game active flag value indicating active gameplay state\n\nDecompiler Discrepancies:\n  extraout_var and CONCAT31 are decompiler artifacts from bool return handling.\n  The actual assembly simply tests EAX for zero after IsGameInMenuState call.",
