      "comment": "Render interactive player character selection slots in the create character screen UI.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load player linked list head pointer from g_pLinkedListHead\n2. Get first player node from list\n3. Check for duplicate player IDs - if none, handle create new character:\n   - Initialize UI state via Ordinal_10034 (clears viewport)\n   - Get current mouse coordinates via FUN_6fb8f680 (x) / FUN_6fb8f690 (y)\n   - If mouse within create button bounds (x: g_dwUIXOffset+0xe3..0x118, y: 0x18..0x23):\n     - Clear UI panel via FUN_6fb31570 with resource 0x1018\n     - Initialize 100 Unicode buffers (200 bytes each) via default constructor\n     - Load string data via Ordinal_10004 and strcpy\n     - Render text via Ordinal_10121, Ordinal_10117\n4. Iterate through each player slot in linked list:\n   - Get slot position offsets from node (+0x4 x offset, +0x8 y offset)\n   - Calculate absolute screen position (add g_dwUIXOffset/g_dwUIYOffset)\n   - Get current mouse coordinates\n   - If mouse within slot bounds (x: ±0x35, y: -0x14..0) AND slot empty:\n     - Render appropriate slot UI based on slot state field (+0x18):\n       - State 0: resource IDs 0x1004/0x1005\n       - State 2: resource IDs 0x1006/0x1007\n       - State 4: resource IDs 0x1008/0x1009\n     - Initialize Unicode buffers and render text strings for slot\n   - Move to next node via offset +0xcd\n   - Continue until end of list\n\nParameters:\nNone (accesses global player list)\n\nReturns:\nvoid\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0xe3, 0x118: Create button x-coordinate bounds\n- 0x18, 0x23: Create button y-coordinate bounds\n- 0x35: Slot width in pixels\n- 0x14: Slot height offset\n- 0x32, 0x37, 0x41: Y-coordinate offsets from viewport\n- 0x1018, 0x1004, 0x1005, 0x1006, 0x1007, 0x1008, 0x1009: UI resource IDs\n- 100: Unicode buffer element count (200 bytes = 100 wide chars)\n- 0x4, 0x8, 0x14, 0x18, 0xcd: Structure field offsets\n- 0xd5, 0xda: Color/style parameters\n\nNote: Function contains SSA temporaries (uVar5, uVar4, iVar1, iVar2, pUVar3, iVar3) that Ghidra optimizes away in decompilation.",
