      "comment": "Format Diablo II resource stat difference string for display in UI.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize 64-element Unicode buffer for output string\n2. Get stat index bitmask from Ordinal_11044(dwStatIndex, dwSecondaryIndex)\n3. Load primary and secondary stat deltas from pConfig + offset\n4. If all three values (bitmask, primary, secondary) are zero, return 0\n5. Apply bit shift using config shift value (pConfig[0x128] & 0x1f)\n6. Check if primary or secondary scaled values are zero:\n   - If both zero: use resource format string (\"+0\")\n   - If equal and positive: use resource format (\"value\")\n   - If equal and negative: use localization config format\n   - If unequal: use \"%d %d\" format with both values\n7. Format selected string using wsprintfA into stack buffer\n8. Convert formatted ANSI to Unicode using toUnicode(pLocalUnicode, szBuffer, 0x20)\n9. Call Ordinal_10004 to initialize display context\n10. Concatenate stat string to UI element 3 times using strcat\n11. Return 1 (success)\n\nParameters:\n  pUnit (ECX): Unit pointer (unit type or index) - context for stat lookup\n  dwStatIndex (EDX): Stat index bitmask - selects which damage/AC/life stat\n  pConfig (Stack[0x4]): Configuration table pointer with offsets:\n    [0x128]: Bit shift amount for scaling (max 0x1f bits)\n    [0x12c]: Offset to primary stat value (damage, AC)\n    [0x130]: Offset to secondary stat value (secondary damage)\n  dwSecondaryIndex (Stack[0x8]): Secondary stat index - used with Ordinal_11044\n\nReturns:\n  1 if string was formatted and concatenated to UI\n  0 if all stat values are zero (no change needed)\n\nMagic Numbers:\n  0x128: Config shift amount field offset\n  0x12c: Primary stat value offset in config table\n  0x130: Secondary stat value offset in config table\n  0x80: Stack frame size (ESP - 0x80)\n  0x20: Unicode buffer element count (64 elements * 2 bytes per element)\n  0x1f: Bit mask for shift amount (limits shift to 0-31)\n  0x8: Right shift amount for