      "comment": "Formats monster damage statistics for tooltip display.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Call FormatLocalizationString with initial parameters to set up base tooltip\n2. Get monster treasure class using GetMonsterTreasureClass(pMonsterData[0], nDifficulty)\n3. Calculate scaled damage: dwTreasureClass * nDifficulty + pMonsterData[0x56]\n4. Get monster max damage using GetMonsterMaxDamage(pMonsterData[0], nDifficulty)\n5. Read base min damage from pMonsterData[0x51]\n6. Read shift amount byte from pMonsterData[0x4a] (single byte at offset 0x128)\n7. Read base max damage from pMonsterData[0x52]\n8. Call FormatResourceString(nScaledDamage, 0) to format damage value\n9. Calculate scaled min: (nBaseDamageMin + nMaxDamage) << shift * nScaledDamage >> 8\n10. Calculate scaled max: (nBaseDamageMax + nMaxDamage) << shift * nScaledDamage >> 8\n11. Call FormatLocalizationString with scaled min/max values, mode=5, param5=0\n12. Return initial result + 1\n\nParameters:\n- pTooltipBuffer (void* in ECX): Output buffer for formatted tooltip string\n- nFormatMode (int in EDX): Format mode selector for localization\n- pMonsterData (int*): Pointer to monster data structure containing:\n    +0x00: Monster index/type ID\n    +0x128 (0x4a*4): Shift amount byte for damage scaling\n    +0x144 (0x51*4): Base minimum damage value\n    +0x148 (0x52*4): Base maximum damage value  \n    +0x158 (0x56*4): Damage offset/bonus value\n- nDifficulty (int): Game difficulty level (0=Normal, 1=Nightmare, 2=Hell)\n- nLevel (int): Monster level for damage calculation\n\nReturns:\n- int: Initial format result + 1 (success indicator)\n\nStructure Layout (pMonsterData offsets):\nOffset  Size  Field         Type   Description\n0x000   4     nMonsterId    int    Monster type identifier\n0x128   1     byShiftAmt    byte   Bit shift for damage scaling\n0x144   4     nDamageMin    int    Base minimum damage\n0x148   4     nDamageMax    int    Base maximum damage\n0x158   4     nDamageBonus  int    Damage offset/bonus\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0x4a, 0x51, 0x52, 0x56: DWORD indices into monster data structure\n- 0x128, 0x144, 0x148, 0x158: Byte offsets (indices * 4)\n- 0x1f: Mask for shift amount (limits to 0-31)\n- 0x8 (>> 8): Division by 256 for fixed-point scaling\n- 5: Format mode for damage range display\n- 0: Suffix type for FormatResourceString",
