      "comment": "Place item in inventory slot with proper initialization and mode setting\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Validate item record (non-zero check)\n2. Look up owner unit by ID at offset +0x2f\n3. Attempt to find existing item in hash table (type 4=item)\n4. If item not found:\n   - Initialize new item with properties from record (offsets +0xb, +0xf, +0x11, +0x13)\n   - Return initialized item pointer\n5. If item found (existing inventory item):\n   - Get owner stat from owner unit (+0x84)\n   - Check if item currently assigned to owner (Ordinal_10262)\n   - If assigned and matches current item, unassign it (Ordinal_10261, FUN_6fb8f0c0)\n   - Query owner's available stat (Ordinal_10754 with stat flag at +0x21)\n   - If stat available:\n     - Remove old item from hash table\n     - Initialize new item with updated properties\n     - Return initialized item\n6. For standalone placement (no owner conflict):\n   - Extract placement mode (byte at +0x3)\n   - Extract width/height from record (+0x11, +0x13)\n   - Find room for item if width or height specified (FindRoomForUnit)\n   - Set item mode via SetItemMode\n   - Set display color via Ordinal_10720\n7. After successful placement:\n   - Check item for stat at +0x36 (via Ordinal_10480)\n   - If stat exists:\n     - Clear stat from item (Ordinal_10486)\n     - Copy stat to owner (Ordinal_10474)\n     - Update item state (Ordinal_10485)\n     - Refresh item display (Ordinal_10376)\n\nParameters:\npItemRecord (int) - Pointer to item placement request record\n  Offset +0x3: Display mode (byte)\n  Offset +0xb: Item ID (dword)\n  Offset +0xf: Item property field 1 (word)\n  Offset +0x11: Item width (word)\n  Offset +0x13: Item height (word)\n  Offset +0x21: Owner stat ID (dword)\n  Offset +0x2d: Owner type (byte)\n  Offset +0x2f: Owner ID (dword)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Item placed successfully, state updated in-place\n\nSpecial Cases:\nNULL record results in fatal error (Ordinal_10023 + FUN_6fb9a10f with -0x1)\nInitialize failure results in fatal error with different error string\nCONCAT22 macro combines two 16-bit values to 32-bit",
