      "comment": "Initializes screen display state and captures frame timestamp for UI rendering.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Extract resource ID from pScreenData->offset 0xc (lower 16-bit word)\n2. Store resource ID in global g_wResourceID\n3. Extract resource flags from pScreenData->offset 0xc (upper 16-bit word via SHR 0x10)\n4. Store resource flags in global g_wResourceFlags\n5. Call GetTickCount() to capture current system time\n6. Store timestamp in global g_dwLastFrameTimestamp\n7. Load global g_dwScreenState for conditional test\n8. Compare g_dwScreenState against special value 5\n9. If equal: set g_nSelectedScreen = 5, g_dwScreenState = 1, g_nScreenParam = 0\n10. Clear pScreenData->offset 0x18 to zero (cleanup flag)\n11. Return to caller\n\nParameters:\npScreenData (UIObject *) - Pointer to UI screen structure containing resource info at offset 0xc and control field at offset 0x18\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Modifies global screen state variables and zero-clears offset 0x18 field before returning\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- State value 5 triggers immediate transition: screen->5, state->1, param->0\n- Non-state-5 execution path also zeros the 0x18 field\n- GetTickCount() executed unconditionally for frame timing reference\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x0c - Resource ID + Flags packed field offset\n0x10 - Bit shift for upper word extraction (flags from combined 32-bit value)\n0x18 - Control flag field offset (cleared before return)\n0x05 - Special screen state value triggering transition\n0x01 - Reset state value after transition",
