      "instructions": ["0xC43E0|SUB|ESP, 0x2c4", "0xC43E6|PUSH|EBX", "0xC43E7|PUSH|EBP", "0xC43E8|PUSH|ESI", "0xC43E9|MOV|ESI, ECX", "0xC43EB|PUSH|EDI", "0xC43EC|MOV|ECX, 0x40", "0xC43F1|XOR|EAX, EAX", "0xC43F3|LEA|EDI, [ESP + 0x1d1]", "0xC43FA|MOV|byte ptr [ESP + 0x1d0], 0x0", "0xC4402|MOV|EBP, EDX", "0xC4404|STOSD.REP|ES:EDI", "0xC4406|STOSW|ES:EDI", "0xC4408|STOSB|ES:EDI", "0xC4409|MOV|EAX, dword ptr [ESP + 0x2d8]"],
      "comment": "Initialize a COF (Component Object File) data unit for a given monster/NPC token.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize large local buffer (0x100 bytes) to zero\n2. Store input monster type and token type for reference\n3. Assert monster type is non-zero (param_1 != 0)\n4. Call Ordinal_10647 to validate token at index 0, exit if invalid\n5. Extract RGB color components from token at index 0, validate character data\n6. Call Ordinal_10647 to validate token at index 1, exit if invalid\n7. Extract RGB color components from token at index 1\n8. Apply token type special handling: if token type == 0 or 0xC, use hardcoded color (0x6874 68) unless monster has custom color (offset 0x2b4)\n9. If global COF token mapping table exists (g_nCofTokenMappingCount > 0), search mapping array for token type match and override color data if found\n10. Apply color mask (0x20 == space becomes 0) to all three color bytes to form masked color values\n11. Format filename using wsprintfA with format \"s_%s_%s_COF_%s_%s_%s_COF\" and color values\n12. Find dot (period) in constructed filename, extract extension portion before last backslash\n13. Copy extension to buffer (excluding dot), validate extension length <= 7\n14. Hash filename extension using Ordinal_502, lookup in hash table g_pCofComponent2TableStart using xor-based hash function\n15. Search linked list of COF data structures for matching filename and buffer\n16. If match found in linked list, reuse existing COF data unit; if not found, allocate new COF data unit\n17. Validate allocated COF data contains valid component data pointer (offset +20)\n18. Extract component header data and validate component count\n19. For each component in buffer: validate component index < 0x10, then for each variation loop\n20. Call FUN_6fb96920 to render COF component, passing component index, color masks, and parameter data\n21. Based on fRenderMode flag (param_4), either disable rendering (Ordinal_10055 with 0,0) or apply special transform (Ordinal_10055 with 1,1) and call Ordinal_10056 if needed\n22. Continue through all components and variations\n\nParameters:\nnMonsterType (int, ECX) - Monster/NPC unit type identifier, must be non-zero\nnTokenType (int, EDX) - Token/animation type index for monster appearance\npTokenData (dword *, stack+4) - Pointer to token data structure array\nfRenderMode (int, stack+8) - Rendering mode flag: 0=normal, 1=special transform\n\nReturns: void (no return value)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If nMonsterType == 0, triggers assertion failure and process termination via FUN_6fb9a10f\n- If nTokenType token lookup fails (Ordinal_10647 returns 0), triggers assertion and termination\n- Global COF token mapping can override color values based on nTokenType\n- Color byte == 0x20 (space character) becomes 0x00 in final representation\n- Extension field of filename must be <= 7 characters, longer strings trigger assertion\n- Hash table lookup may fail (pdVar5 == NULL) which triggers COF allocation branch\n- Multiple component variations handled in nested loop structure\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x40 - Buffer initialization loop count (256 bytes / 4 bytes per iteration = 64 iterations)\n0x2b4 - Monster unit offset for custom color flag check\n0x1e0 - Monster unit offset for custom color RGB data\n0x20 - Space character ASCII value, used as color mask comparand\n0x10 - Maximum component index (16 valid components)\n0x1ff0000, 0x1ff00, 0x1ff - Hash function mask constants for component table lookup\n0xd3b, 0xd43, 0xd46 - Assertion failure line numbers\n0x6fc04db0 - Component table/database identifier\n\nNote: Function uses 21 stack-allocated temporaries optimized away by compiler (phantom variables).",
