      "comment": "Format floating-point number to decimal or scientific notation string representation.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Call FUN_6fba1c35 to decompose double into sign, significand, and exponent\n2. Call FUN_6fba1bbe to format significand digits into output buffer\n3. Calculate exponent-adjusted character count (nCharCount - 1)\n4. If exponent < -4 or > buffer size, use scientific notation (FUN_6fba1c35)\n5. Otherwise, use decimal notation with decimal point insertion:\n   - Find null terminator in output string\n   - Backspace one character (remove trailing zero)\n   - Call FUN_6fba1c35 to insert decimal point at correct position\n6. Return formatted string in szOutput\n\nParameters:\n- pdblValue: Pointer to 64-bit IEEE double to format\n- szOutput: Buffer for output string (null-terminated on return)\n- cbBuffer: Size of szOutput buffer in bytes\n- nFlags: Formatting flags (controls precision, notation type)\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Output written to szOutput buffer\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0x2d: ASCII '-' (minus sign character)\n- 0x20: Bit 5 (sign flag indicator from decomposition)\n- -4: Threshold for switching to scientific notation (exponent below -4 forces E notation)\n- 0x1: Flag passed to formatting functions (enable sign handling)\n- 0x0: Null terminator for string termination\n\nRelated Functions:\n- __cfltcvt: Caller, main float-to-string conversion orchestrator\n- FUN_6fba1c35: Decompose IEEE double into components\n- FUN_6fba1bbe: Format significand as decimal digits\n- FormatNumberWithDecimal: Insert decimal point for fixed-point notation\n- FormatScientificNotation: Format with exponent for very large/small values",
