      "comment": "Initialize character type table for a specific code page (locale).\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Acquire critical section lock (0x19) for thread safety\n2. Check if requested code page already initialized via g_dwCurrentCodePage\n3. If not initialized:\n   a. Search g_adwCodePageTable for matching code page entry\n   b. If found in preset table: copy character class masks to g_abCharacterTypeTable\n   c. If not found: call GetCPInfo to retrieve Windows code page info\n   d. Populate g_abCharacterTypeTable with lead byte and character class ranges\n   e. Mark dual-byte support via g_dwCharacterClassMask and lead byte flags\n4. Store code page configuration to globals (g_dwCurrentCodePage, g_dwLeadByteMin/Max)\n5. Release critical section lock\n6. Return success (0) or failure (0xFFFFFFFF)\n\nParameters:\nnCodePage (int): Code page identifier (932=Shift-JIS, 949=EUC-KR, 950=Big5, 1252=Western)\n\nReturns:\n0 (success) - Code page initialized, character table ready\n0xFFFFFFFF (failure) - Invalid code page or system error, no error handler available\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Single-byte code pages (MaxCharSize < 2) skip lead byte processing\n- Thread safety: entire operation protected by critical section lock\n- Preset tables cached for common code pages (performance optimization)\n- Lead byte ranges specified as start-end byte pairs in g_adwCodePageTable\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x19 - Critical section index for locale operations\n0x40 - Word count for clearing character type table (256 bytes / 4)\n0x30 - Entry size in preset table (multiple of 3 DWORDs per code page)\n0xFF - Maximum single-byte character value (last valid index)\n4 - Number of lead byte range pairs processed\n0xFFFFFFF - Error return value",
